\description{
Maximizes the number of matched pairs subject to balance constraints, and
reports how far the matched sample sits from the largest one the constraints
admit.
}
\details{
Cardinality matching (Zubizarreta 2012) asks for the largest matched sample
that satisfies stated balance constraints, rather than the closest one. The
constraints come in two kinds, and which kinds are stated decides how the
problem is solved.

Fine and refined covariate balance are representable in the matching network
itself (Rosenbaum, Ross and Silber 2007; Pimentel, Kelz, Silber and
Rosenbaum 2015): every category becomes a node, and a single min-cost flow
solve returns the largest balanced sample together with a dual certificate
of its optimality, at polynomial cost. \code{max_std_diff} defaults to \code{Inf},
stating no moment constraint, so a call asking for fine or refined balance
alone takes this path and comes back certified:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{cardinality_match(left, right, vars, fine = "region")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Linear moment constraints, bounds on a standardized or mean difference, do
not fit in the network. Summed over the matched pairs, such a bound is a
linear constraint on which units are matched, never on how they are paired,
so \code{engine = "branch_bound"} searches over unit selections: an integer
programme with one 0/1 variable per unit and a few rows, solved by branch and
bound on its linear relaxation. A finite \code{max_std_diff} states one such row
per variable and per direction, so it is what moves a call onto this path.

The objective is lexicographic, and the search settles it one stage at a
time. It first finds the most matched units the constraints admit, then the
least imbalance at each refined level, coarsest first, and last the least
total distance. A pair is never added at the cost of a match and never
dropped to shorten one. The distance stage reads the cheapest pairing of a
selection off the balance network and adds a cut from the potentials of that
solve (Benders decomposition), and when calipers or \code{max_distance} forbid
pairs, a selection whose units cannot all be paired adds a Hall-condition
row instead.

The report says how the search ended. \code{certified} is \code{TRUE} only when every
stage closed, and \code{gap} says how many matched units separate the answer from
the bound when the first stage did not. Every stopping path returns a matched
set that satisfies every stated constraint, together with a bound valid for
the whole problem. When the distance stage stops on the budget, the matched
sample is a largest balanced one, with \code{gap} zero, whose total distance is
the smallest the search reached.

The certificate carries no tolerance. Each bound is a weak-duality bound read
from the relaxation's duals in exact arithmetic or with a rigorous rounding
margin, so it holds whatever the simplex returned, and every selection is
checked against the constraints exactly before it is accepted.

How long the search runs depends on how far the relaxation sits from the
best integer selection. With continuous covariates the relaxation's bound on
the number of matched units is usually within one unit of the optimum, and
the search closes at its first node when it finds a selection that reaches
it. When the optimum lies one unit below that bound, proving it takes many
more nodes, and the report shows the incumbent beside a bound one unit
above it. Set \code{node_limit} and \code{time_limit} to a budget you are willing to
spend, and read \code{stopped_on} and \code{gap} to see what the budget bought.

\code{engine = "heuristic"} runs a pruning loop instead: a full match, then
repeated deletion of the pairs carrying the worst variable's imbalance. Its
properties follow from the loop. It starts from a full match and deletes
pairs, and never re-adds one, so it cannot recover from an over-aggressive
deletion. It never maximizes cardinality, it only shrinks a starting match.
It considers one variable per iteration, the current worst, so balance on
the others is incidental. It computes no bound, so it cannot say how far its
answer sits from the largest balanced sample; \code{best_possible} and \code{gap} are
\code{NA} and \code{certified} is \code{FALSE}.
}
\examples{
set.seed(42)
left <- data.frame(id = 1:20, x = rnorm(20), y = rnorm(20),
                   region = rep(c("A", "B"), length.out = 20))
right <- data.frame(id = 21:50, x = rnorm(30, 0.5), y = rnorm(30, 0.3),
                    region = rep(c("A", "B"), length.out = 30))

# Exact fine balance on region, no moment constraint: one flow solve,
# answered with a certificate.
fit <- cardinality_match(left, right, vars = c("x", "y"),
                         fine = "region")
fit$cardinality

# A standardized-difference bound as well: the same match, searched over
# unit selections under a small node budget.
bb <- cardinality_match(left, right, vars = c("x", "y"),
                        fine = "region", max_std_diff = 0.1,
                        node_limit = 25L)
bb$cardinality

}
